    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "It is quite a process. It is very much inside baseball, and it consumes an immense amount of time at the beginning of a session. Most of the committees are what are called standing committees, which means they exist every Congress. So, for example, Ways and Means is a standing committee. Armed Services is a standing committee. The Commerce Committee, I was on it. It was a standing committee. Government Reform and Oversight, a standing committee, I was on it. Natural Resources, a standing committee. On those committees, at least on the Republican side and the Democrat side, is very similar. You make it known what committee you'd like to be on for some of the committees that are viewed as more powerful than others, and more sought after than others, you, many members literally wage a campaign. In my first term in Congress, I asked to be put on Ways and Means. I was not given that, but I was promised I'd be put on Ways and Means at the beginning of the next Congress. And I was told that the reason I wasn't getting Ways and Means at the first Congress was that I had a relatively solid Republican congressional district and I didn't need it in order to get reelected.",
