    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/7/87091.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 62,
    "turn_text": "off the committee. And Mr. McCarthy heard that view expressed by his conference during his confirmation as speaker. I mean, one of the things we heard was that members of the conference made demands not putting Mr. Schiff back on the intel committee, which is a privilege, it's not a right, was something that they demanded. What that shows me, and I'll just stop here for a second from kind of the momentary issue of the authority to not reappoint Mr. Schiff to the intel committee and look for a moment at Mr. McCarthy. I am thrilled at Mr. McCarthy. Number one is the one you already noted. He, for the first time, right up front, very directly, and in a very public way, proved he has the courage to keep his word, to be honest, and to take tough action. As we saw, the minority leader wanted Schiff back on. He was told he was not going to get back on, yet he still submitted Schiff's name. And, of course, he said, no, you apparently didn't. Listen, I said he's not getting back on, and he's not. And so that was courage and willingness to take a strong stance. It was also the keeping of a promise. He had made a promise, which is why I thought the process that went on while Mr. McCarthy was being selected as speaker could prove to be positive. In this case, it proved to be positive. I don't think Schiff deserves to be on that committee by his conduct. A lot of members in Washington obviously didn't think he deserved to be on. And a number of them were willing to stand up and say, this is one of our demands. And I think the nation's national security will benefit from it. But most importantly, it proves what What we talked here about Mr. McCarthy and that is that he not only has courage, which I was not sure about myself when that was going on, but also that he's a man of his word, which I kind of thought he was, but you don't know until it gets really tough. And here he has proven to be a man of his word. And Lots of speakers that I served under at times broke their work. And I watched it happen where they said at one point, I will not do X. And then months go by and they come back and they do X and you confront them and they tell you, well, circumstance has changed. In this case, Mr. McCarthy made a promise and kept his promise. And I think that's good for the nation and good for Republicans. A point I would make is that and i think i said this earlier the democratic party is very monolithic they all pretty much think alike as a group you look at the republican party and they are more I would say they are more willing to cross their leaders, more willing to think independently, more willing to stand up and say, no, wait a minute, that may be what a majority of my party thinks, but I don't agree on this issue. And that's kind of where I was when I, on more occasions than they liked, I crossed leadership. What has happened here is Mr. McCarthy has realized you can't just listen to some in your conference. You've got to listen to everyone in your conference. And when they make a reasonable demand, you should exceed to that demand. And I think that's what McCarthy did here. And I think it's a very good sign for the future. And I think it demonstrates he has an open mind, which I think is critically important for leaders.",
