    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/7/87091.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 68,
    "turn_text": "I do. It is the media's job to question or challenge or to use your phrase, stand up to all elected leaders, whether they're Republican or Democrat. Sadly, we currently live in an era when the media, by and large in America, and it's very large in America has picked one side and they don't ever ask tough questions or stand up to or challenge members of the Democrat Party or the current Democrat president. But in this instance in the tape you played showed it, a reporter was challenging Mr. McCarthy. And one of the reasons that I am so pleased with Mr. McCarthy and one of the reasons I'm so happy and encouraged today as a Republican is that McCarthy not only stood up But he stood up with respect. I am a huge admirer and fan of Newt Gingrich. And when my kids were little, they had a clubhouse out back that I helped them build. And he put up Newt Gingrich posters all over. because I was running for Congress, and I will always remain a fan of Newt. But Newt, as skilled as he was, had a shortcoming, and that was, and in part it's because he worked so hard, but he would get tired And in a situation like this, he would get short with the press. And he would respond in an angry tone. And sometimes he would say, that's the stupidest question I've heard. Well, you're never going to win anybody over that way. I used to try to point out to nude staffers. I'd ask him a question. When was the last time you ever saw Ronald Reagan lose his temper with a reporter? The answer, of course, which they didn't want to come back with was never. Because Reagan got asked the sixth question over and over again, or the same question for six times, he would come back and say, I'm glad you asked that question. And he'd repeat the same answer he'd given six times before. In this case what was awesome and we heard it in the tape and people can go back and listen or when they watch the news tonight. He said, let me be clear and respectful to you. So he didn't treat the reporter who was in all candor exhibiting her bias but arguably still doing her job he didn't treat her with disrespect and say you have no right to listen to say that or you know how smart are you or why did you ask such a bad question or unfair question he said let me be clear and respectful to you and that's the way you you should treat reporters Unlike doing it out of anger and I think Kevin is very talented at dealing with the press and he's demonstrating that he's good at this job.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The media's job is to question or challenge elected leaders regardless of party affiliation.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "In the tape shown, a reporter challenged Mr. McCarthy.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Mr. McCarthy stood up to the reporter while maintaining respect.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Currently, much of the American media has taken a partisan side against the Democratic Party.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Angry responses toward reporters alienate audiences and fail to persuade.",
